you  [Music]  chef Tony Montano has been satisfying  Chicago's taste for fine Italian food  for more than three decades he was a  music major in college and only started  working in restaurants as a part-time  job the cooking soon became his passion  he started his career in Wisconsin but  found his way to the Windy City and  later spent a year working in top  restaurants throughout Italy bringing  their secrets home to his restaurant  spiaggia a must visit venue on Chicago's  dining scene chef Tony Montano what  welcome to the dish thanks for being  here thank you for the invitation I'm so  excited  I mean besides this our son lives here  in New York so we get to have dinner  with him after this okay thank tell us  what's on this table so if we could  start way on the end over there that's a  drink with like my favorite alcoholic  tequila and Aperol and a little Prosecco  so it's almost like a Mexican Italian  the ground Negroni  and then the next serving for a Saturday  morning the shrimp dish is a dish that  we do at the US Open every year and this  flaming ouzo shrimp is so powerful when  you cook it and you pour the mousse on  light on fire which we won't do here  darn it permeates the entire Arthur Ashe  Stadium I think I may be pushing it but  that's good tennis and then of course  from Chicago we have a big steak and in  the Tuscan tradition of bistecca alla  Fiorentina  and then over here is a ravioli which is  a type pasta that is a sheet then we  wrap around cheese and bake it and now  we have spring vegetables because it's  spring course and then this is my  childhood on the very end this is a what  my grandmother from Calabria used to  call us Khalidi and it's coated in honey  and fried and it's just like oh my god  alight yeah yeah let's talk about your  childhood you are you were gonna be a  musician  it was Rhambo and I hear ya trombones so  it's interesting we did form a band a  brass band yeah like you know was the  days of Chicago and Earth Wind &amp; Fire  tower of power and the other bands that  really influenced us and then years  later when I was on Top Chef Masters  with Jonathan Waxman another trombone  player so I like both chefs and actually  grew up in the Bay Area so he knew you  know  yeah we could have done it yeah what I  gather you're first a meeting this is  the one how did you make that transition  though from the trombone to the kitchen  it was like cold turkey it's like guys  they have been married for 32 years and  my wife Cathy has never seen me play the  trombone ever so okay you were done when  you were done I was done why I wanted to  cook I always wanted to cook I mean if  food was a big part of growing up  everything that we did but that we're  love of food I mean you talk about your  Italian grandmother she must have  imparted a lot of kind of wisdom and  enthusiasm for this kind of food  totally like every holiday we were at  their house they also had the montemagno  food shop which was part of the  italian-american community in Kenosha  Wisconsin so it was all about food he  had it my grandfather had a great guard  and she was a great cook so it's like  yeah I'd like I'd like to continue  eating this well okay well you know we  mentioned that once you get your first  job in Chicago there was time they  actually sent you to Italy for a year  right yes what was that like to be well  I mean you know this is back in the  early 80s no you know no internet like  if you were going someplace you had a  map all right I remember it was a lot  different but really what they told us  there when we were working those  restaurants are like wow you guys are  like the first Americans that have come  here to learn a lot of times American  restaurant tours will call Italy and say  hey I need an Italian chef but you're  like doing it really different so they  they really like that and they really  want to make sure we learn so they took  a lot of attention when you clearly  learned and now you're in Chicago which  is one of the great restaurant cities in  America these days yeah we love and  spiaggia it's got a great location right  on Oak in Michigan and across from the  beach and we have fine dining admission  star we have a cafe and we have great  spots for private dining both in the  restaurant and in our private event  space dominating this mean more than 30  years believe it or not chef as I asked  you to sign this dish as a system on  this program I'll ask you the question  we always ask which is if you could  share this bounty with any figure past  or present who would it be  so my namesake so my father had an older  brother named Tony montemagno who  actually was lost at Iwo Jima  world war two but the thing was he was  an incredible musician I think like  maybe I may be where you got yeah I  never met to meet Yemen cook for him to  tonymont to Ana's one table I loved it  chef Tony Montano great to see you thank  you and congrats on all the success  thank you for more on chef Tony Montano  and the dish head to our website at CBS  this morning calm

